1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,352
A watermelon full of seeds.

2
00:00:02,352 --> 00:00:04,675
A cricket ball in a huge stadium.

3
00:00:04,675 --> 00:00:07,098
A staircase you climb step by step.

4
00:00:07,498 --> 00:00:09,128
Three ordinary pictures.

5
00:00:09,128 --> 00:00:12,067
Each was once our best
picture of the atom.

6
00:00:12,517 --> 00:00:14,890
Diya cuts a watermelon in her kitchen.

7
00:00:14,890 --> 00:00:17,171
Black seeds sit all through the red.

8
00:00:17,521 --> 00:00:21,991
In eighteen ninety eight, JJ Thomson
pictured the atom just like this.

9
00:00:22,491 --> 00:00:26,480
By the end, you will see why it
broke, and what replaced it, twice.

10
00:00:27,130 --> 00:00:29,066
So what is an atomic model?

11
00:00:29,416 --> 00:00:31,991
A picture that explains the experiments.

12
00:00:31,991 --> 00:00:34,821
When one disagrees, the model must change.

13
00:00:35,171 --> 00:00:36,325
Thomson's model.

14
00:00:36,325 --> 00:00:41,475
The atom is a sphere, radius about ten
to the minus ten metres, with positive

15
00:00:41,475 --> 00:00:43,690
charge spread evenly through it.

16
00:00:44,040 --> 00:00:47,359
Electrons sit inside, like
seeds in a watermelon.

17
00:00:47,359 --> 00:00:50,425
The charges balance,
so the atom is neutral.

18
00:00:50,775 --> 00:00:52,923
And the mass is spread evenly too.

19
00:00:52,923 --> 00:00:54,698
That is about to be tested.

20
00:00:55,348 --> 00:00:59,970
On our molecule bench, Rutherford
fires fast alpha particles at a gold

21
00:00:59,970 --> 00:01:03,217
foil, only about one
hundred nanometres thick.

22
00:01:03,567 --> 00:01:06,758
An alpha particle carries
two positive charges.

23
00:01:06,758 --> 00:01:10,889
A zinc sulphide screen around
the foil flashes at every hit.

24
00:01:11,290 --> 00:01:14,878
If Thomson was right, all of them
should pass almost straight through.

25
00:01:15,278 --> 00:01:16,187
Most did.

26
00:01:16,187 --> 00:01:18,358
A few bent by small angles.

27
00:01:18,758 --> 00:01:22,335
But about one in twenty thousand
bounced almost straight back.

28
00:01:22,985 --> 00:01:24,013
Link by link.

29
00:01:24,013 --> 00:01:27,594
Most pass straight, so most
of the atom is empty space.

30
00:01:27,994 --> 00:01:28,969
A few bend.

31
00:01:28,969 --> 00:01:33,676
So the positive charge is packed into
one tiny place, pushing them away.

32
00:01:34,076 --> 00:01:35,712
A very few bounce back.

33
00:01:35,712 --> 00:01:38,588
So that place holds
almost all the mass too.

34
00:01:38,588 --> 00:01:40,705
Rutherford called it the nucleus.

35
00:01:41,105 --> 00:01:44,269
Nucleus, about ten to
the minus fifteen metres.

36
00:01:44,269 --> 00:01:46,677
Atom, about ten to the minus ten.

37
00:01:46,677 --> 00:01:51,245
If the nucleus were a cricket ball,
the atom's radius would be about five

38
00:01:51,245 --> 00:01:52,145
kilometres.

39
00:01:52,751 --> 00:01:57,818
Rutherford's model: a tiny, heavy,
positive nucleus, with electrons circling

40
00:01:57,818 --> 00:01:59,116
it like planets.

41
00:01:59,766 --> 00:02:00,877
Side by side.

42
00:02:00,877 --> 00:02:03,355
Thomson spreads charge and mass out.

43
00:02:03,355 --> 00:02:06,204
Rutherford packs them into a tiny nucleus.

44
00:02:06,604 --> 00:02:07,794
Pause and think.

45
00:02:07,794 --> 00:02:11,367
Which result alone proves that
Thomson's picture was wrong?

46
00:02:14,567 --> 00:02:16,376
The ones that bounced back.

47
00:02:16,376 --> 00:02:19,813
Spread out charge is far too
weak to turn them around.

48
00:02:19,813 --> 00:02:21,965
Only a tiny, heavy nucleus can.

49
00:02:22,615 --> 00:02:24,301
But there was a problem.

50
00:02:24,301 --> 00:02:28,493
A circling electron keeps changing
direction, so it is accelerating.

51
00:02:28,893 --> 00:02:31,438
An accelerating charge radiates energy.

52
00:02:31,438 --> 00:02:35,938
The electron would spiral into the
nucleus in about one hundred millionth

53
00:02:35,938 --> 00:02:36,987
of a second.

54
00:02:37,387 --> 00:02:38,774
Atoms would collapse.

55
00:02:38,774 --> 00:02:40,172
But they are stable.

56
00:02:40,172 --> 00:02:41,812
The model had to change.

57
00:02:42,462 --> 00:02:46,282
In nineteen thirteen, Niels
Bohr fixed it with four rules.

58
00:02:46,632 --> 00:02:47,532
Rule one.

59
00:02:47,503 --> 00:02:52,454
The electron moves only in fixed circular
orbits, called stationary states,

60
00:02:52,454 --> 00:02:54,079
without losing energy.

61
00:02:54,829 --> 00:02:55,766
Rule two.

62
00:02:55,766 --> 00:03:01,134
Each orbit has a fixed energy: E n
equals minus two point one eight times

63
00:03:01,134 --> 00:03:04,949
ten to the minus eighteen
joules, over n squared.

64
00:03:05,699 --> 00:03:06,633
Rule three.

65
00:03:06,633 --> 00:03:11,294
To jump between orbits, the electron
takes in or gives out a photon, a

66
00:03:11,294 --> 00:03:14,422
packet of light, whose
energy equals the gap.

67
00:03:15,172 --> 00:03:16,190
Rule four.

68
00:03:16,190 --> 00:03:21,046
Only orbits with m v r equal
to n h over two pi are allowed.

69
00:03:21,046 --> 00:03:25,973
Each radius is fifty two point
nine picometres, times n squared.

70
00:03:26,723 --> 00:03:27,773
Remember this.

71
00:03:27,773 --> 00:03:31,855
In Bohr's atom, energy comes in
fixed steps, like a staircase.

72
00:03:32,555 --> 00:03:34,290
Now, one worked example.

73
00:03:34,590 --> 00:03:35,490
Hydrogen.

74
00:03:35,434 --> 00:03:38,920
The electron jumps from
orbit one to orbit two.

75
00:03:39,420 --> 00:03:40,376
Orbit one.

76
00:03:40,376 --> 00:03:45,707
E one is minus two point one eight
times ten to the minus eighteen joules.

77
00:03:46,457 --> 00:03:47,414
Orbit two.

78
00:03:47,414 --> 00:03:50,295
Divide by two squared, which is four.

79
00:03:50,295 --> 00:03:55,300
E two is minus zero point five four
five times ten to the minus eighteen

80
00:03:55,300 --> 00:03:56,200
joules.

81
00:03:56,776 --> 00:04:01,883
The gap, E two minus E one, is one
point six three five times ten to the

82
00:04:01,883 --> 00:04:03,488
minus eighteen joules.

83
00:04:04,238 --> 00:04:09,192
Radius: fifty two point nine times
four, two hundred and eleven point six

84
00:04:09,192 --> 00:04:10,102
picometres.

85
00:04:10,752 --> 00:04:12,218
Now, the exam trap.

86
00:04:12,568 --> 00:04:17,272
Some students compare two point one
eight with zero point five four five,

87
00:04:17,272 --> 00:04:19,707
and say orbit one has more energy.

88
00:04:20,107 --> 00:04:21,570
Watch the minus sign.

89
00:04:21,570 --> 00:04:25,930
Minus two point one eight is lower,
so orbit one is the most stable.

90
00:04:26,580 --> 00:04:28,169
Where is this asked?

91
00:04:28,169 --> 00:04:32,861
As statements on Rutherford's observations,
and numericals on Bohr's energy

92
00:04:32,861 --> 00:04:33,842
and radius.

93
00:04:34,442 --> 00:04:35,752
Exam level now.

94
00:04:35,752 --> 00:04:40,684
A hydrogen electron has an energy of
minus two point four two times ten

95
00:04:40,684 --> 00:04:42,905
to the minus nineteen joules.

96
00:04:42,905 --> 00:04:45,427
What is the radius of its orbit?

97
00:04:45,427 --> 00:04:46,793
Pause and solve.

98
00:04:52,193 --> 00:04:53,602
The answer is B.

99
00:04:53,602 --> 00:04:57,131
Four hundred and seventy
six point one picometres.

100
00:04:57,631 --> 00:05:02,444
Two point one eight times ten to the
minus eighteen, over two point four

101
00:05:02,444 --> 00:05:05,736
two times ten to the
minus nineteen, is nine.

102
00:05:05,736 --> 00:05:07,414
So n squared is nine.

103
00:05:07,914 --> 00:05:11,335
Radius is fifty two point
nine times n squared.

104
00:05:11,335 --> 00:05:13,743
Fifty two point nine times nine.

105
00:05:14,243 --> 00:05:18,216
That is four hundred and seventy
six point one picometres.

106
00:05:18,216 --> 00:05:20,240
A used n, not n squared.

107
00:05:20,240 --> 00:05:21,660
C used orbit two.

108
00:05:22,260 --> 00:05:23,724
Here is your recap.

109
00:05:23,724 --> 00:05:25,228
Screenshot this card.

110
00:05:28,428 --> 00:05:31,265
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

111
00:05:31,665 --> 00:05:32,565
One.

112
00:05:32,231 --> 00:05:35,718
Why did Thomson's model
fail the gold foil test?

113
00:05:36,518 --> 00:05:37,418
Two.

114
00:05:37,029 --> 00:05:39,435
Why should Rutherford's atom collapse?

115
00:05:40,235 --> 00:05:41,135
Three.

116
00:05:40,886 --> 00:05:44,838
Which orbit of hydrogen has
the lowest energy, and why?

117
00:05:45,738 --> 00:05:50,313
Next time: why does glowing hydrogen
give only a few sharp colours?

118
00:05:50,313 --> 00:05:54,091
We find out in atomic spectra
and the hydrogen spectrum.
